==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|en-|life|-en}} {{q|intensifying verbal [[circumfix]]}}.<ref name="OED-etym"> The '''Oxford English Dictionary''' (2007) states that '''enliven''' was formed by the simultaneous prefixing of {{m|en|en-}} and suffixing of {{m|en|-en}}, which, by definition, constitutes [[circumfix]]ation.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɛnˈlaɪvən/|a=RP}}
*: {{rhymes|en|aɪvən|s=3}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-enliven.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To give [[life]] or [[spirit]] to; to [[revive]] or [[animate]].
#: {{syn|en|inspirit|invigorate|quicken|vitalise|vivify}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make more [[lively]], [[cheerful]] or [[interesting]].
#: {{syn|en|cheer up|liven|liven up|quicken|Thesaurus:enliven}}
#: {{ux|en|The game was much '''enlivened''' when both teams scored within five minutes of each other.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=May|title=A Tunisian Electric Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=335|text=Travel is '''enlivened''' not only by the mixed company of French, Berbers, Arabs, and descendants of the Mediterranean-Corsairs who sit together indiscriminately, but also by itinerant vendors of macaroons, sweetmeats and the like, who, as long as they have a travel ticket, ply their wares unhindered by the [ticket] collectors.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-08-07|author=Kieran Pender|title=Caitlin Foord and Hayley Raso fire Australia into quarter-finals with win over Denmark|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/aug/07/matildas-australia-denmark-last-16-women-s-world-cup-match-report|passage=The pace came off the match as the first half continued, with the occasional frenetic moment down the flanks '''enlivening''' what otherwise became a chess-like tactical battle.}}

